WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
The first film used a middle marker to expose double crossovers.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.100
Do those doubles occur as often as independent intervals predict?

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.380
Keep the same blue and gold chromosomes, green middle marker, and red crossover

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.380
points.

5
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:22.107
Treat a crossover in each interval as an event.

6
00:00:23.427 --> 00:00:28.227
Under independence, multiply the two interval frequencies to predict double

7
00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:33.027
crossovers. The product fills an expected bar, marked schematic.

8
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.610
Now compare an observed bar from the offspring with that expectation.

9
00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.850
Suppose the observed bar is shorter.

10
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.550
Does that mean positive or negative interference?

11
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:54.893
Fewer doubles appeared than independence predicted.

12
00:00:56.073 --> 00:01:01.693
That is positive interference: a crossover in one interval is associated with fewer

13
00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:07.853
in its neighbour. The chromosome picture shows association, not a molecular cause.

14
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:14.107
The coefficient of coincidence is observed doubles divided by expected doubles.

15
00:01:15.427 --> 00:01:17.747
Interference is the complement of coincidence.

16
00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:24.287
Keep the pair together: coincidence measures what appeared; interference measures

17
00:01:24.287 --> 00:01:25.727
the departure from independence.

18
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.390
Three comparisons organize the signs.

19
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.570
Observed below expected gives positive interference.

20
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:36.790
Equal bars give no interference.

21
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.690
Observed above expected gives negative interference, an excess of doubles.

22
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.910
That has been reported in some fungal and phage crosses.

23
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.090
No single value holds everywhere.

24
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:55.653
Interference usually weakens as the intervals lie farther apart.

25
00:01:56.993 --> 00:02:00.353
Observed doubles then approach the independent expectation.

26
00:02:01.613 --> 00:02:06.533
So the measurement belongs to one pair of intervals in one organism, not to a whole

27
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:07.533
species.

28
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.687
One cross gives an estimate, not a law.

29
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:17.047
A zero observed bar may reflect strong interference, a small sample, or missing

30
00:02:17.047 --> 00:02:23.207
classes. Check survival and scoring, then collect more offspring before claiming

31
00:02:23.207 --> 00:02:24.207
complete interference.

32
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:30.230
Doubles have answered one question: how crossovers are spaced relative to each

33
00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.650
other. A second question is where along the DNA they fall.

34
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.610
Answering it needs a second ruler.

35
00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:41.453
A genetic map records crossover frequency.

36
00:02:42.873 --> 00:02:45.193
A DNA ruler records physical length.

37
00:02:46.353 --> 00:02:48.673
Align them, and their spacing can disagree.

38
00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:55.253
Crossovers are not spread evenly, so equal DNA lengths can have different genetic

39
00:02:55.253 --> 00:02:56.253
distances.

40
00:02:56.967 --> 00:03:00.767
In humans and mice, many crossovers cluster in hotspots.

41
00:03:02.087 --> 00:03:07.047
A hotspot is a short DNA stretch with more recombination than nearby DNA.

42
00:03:08.407 --> 00:03:13.547
The text below explains how hotspot positions are chosen and why that scope is not

43
00:03:13.547 --> 00:03:14.547
universal.

44
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.980
Maps also change with the gamete maker and chromosome region.

45
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.940
Female and male maps can differ.

46
00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.980
Some regions suppress crossovers.

47
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.080
These patterns move the genetic marks without moving the underlying DNA sequence.

48
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.740
Return to the two bars.

49
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:40.140
Their comparison turns a hidden event into a spacing test, with scope limited to

50
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.140
this interval pair.

51
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.320
Comparing observed and expected doubles reveals crossover spacing.
